hi could I ask my cinematographer Madeon  to come up and join me so the North Bay  fire swept through Northern California's  wine country during my first month of  grad school at the time it was the  deadliest natural disaster in  California's history they burned  uncontrolled for 23 days and we heard  story after story of the physical  devastation that they left behind but  what I heard less of were the stories of  those who live in the shadows of wine  country the 50,000 undocumented  immigrants who make up the backbone of  Napa and Sonoma as tourism and  agricultural industries because of their  immigration status they were left most  vulnerable after the fires when I set  out to make this film a year later it  was their story that I wanted to tell so  first and foremost I want to thank  Consuela Medrano who is a heroine in  this film and in her community in Napa  Gustavo Vargas and all of the people who  had to remain anonymous but were brave  enough to trust me with their story I'd  like to thank everyone at the UC  Berkeley Graduate School of Journalism  who made this film possible my advisers  Carrie Lozano Don Porter  Cassandra Herman Lauren caps Chris O'Day  and Mike Shen my fearless  cinematographer Mary Ann Curtis kettle  who made this film beautiful  and everyone in the 2019 documentary  class who provided endless feedback and  moral support thank you thank you to  Andrew ching jacob bloomfield miss ratch  and william Fritsch who composed the  music for the film and jim Labrecque and  berkeley sound artist whom artists who  mixed it I'd like to thank my parents  and my brother who are here tonight  thank you for believing me and  supporting me every step of the way and  last but not least I never thought I  would say this but thank you to pick out  of me thank you so much for this  incredible honor  [Applause]
 

